<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Cook Islands</h1><p class="st-meta">1901 CE – present · capital Avarua</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Polynesian island group annexed by New Zealand in 1901 that has governed itself in free association with New Zealand since 1965.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>With the consent of leading ariki, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> annexed the islands of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cook-islands-federation">Cook Islands Federation</a>, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niue">Niue</a>, on 11 June 1901. Niue was soon placed under a separate administration. A resident commissioner at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avarua">Avarua</a> on <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-rarotonga">Rarotonga</a> governed on behalf of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> government.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Colonial administration was paternalistic, with the land court and island councils replacing the federation's institutions, and the islands depended on exports of citrus, copra and pearl shell to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>. Cook Islanders served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and the 1918 influenza struck <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-rarotonga">Rarotonga</a>. Large-scale emigration to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> began after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and Cook Islanders are New Zealand citizens.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>In 1965 the islands became self-governing in free association with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> under Premier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-henry">Albert Henry</a>, who was removed in 1978 for electoral fraud. The Cook Islands conducts much of its own foreign policy and relies on tourism, pearls and remittances.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/cook-islands.jpg" alt="Cook Islands" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Cook_Islands.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1901 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Avarua</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Albert Henry, Tom Davis</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Protestantism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Cook Islands Māori, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/cook-islands">Cook Islands</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cook Islands (New Zealand)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1964&amp;focus=cook-islands" aria-label="Cook Islands on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="386 2962 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Cook Islands lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M706 3218L706 3217M706 3218L9384 3761M706 3218L9268 3906M706 3218L9268 3906"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M706 3217h0M9384 3761h0M9268 3906h0M9268 3906h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M706 3218h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M706 3218h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Avarua. <a href="/atlas?year=1964&amp;focus=cook-islands">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
